    "html": "<p>A voice assistant sounds like one feature. In practice it is a pipeline: capture speech, work out when the person has finished, transcribe it, generate a reply, turn that into audio, and play it back while the conversation is still moving.</p>\n<p>I wanted to understand how the structure of that pipeline changes what you have to run, so I built it on two architectures.</p>\n<h2>The first attempt was not a conversation</h2>\n<p>My first proof of concept recorded about three seconds of audio and uploaded it to a <code>POST</code> endpoint. It worked, in the sense that audio went in and audio came out. But a fixed recording window cannot behave like a conversation, so I moved it to a legacy folder and made LiveKit the supported transport.</p>\n<h2>Stack one: everything local and inspectable</h2>\n<p>The browser publishes mic audio to a LiveKit room and sends small rolling chunks to an Express server. A Flask service transcribes them with faster-whisper, Ollama writes a short reply, Sarvam generates an MP3, and a LiveKit data packet tells the browser to play it.</p>\n<p>Every stage has an output you can look at, which makes it easy to debug. The cost is a Python sidecar and several processes to keep running.</p>\n<h2>Stack two: a worker in the room</h2>\n<p>Here Express only serves health checks, tokens and the client page. A LiveKit Agents worker does the rest: Silero VAD finds the end of each utterance, AssemblyAI transcribes it, the LLM replies, and audio is published straight back into the room.</p>\n<p>There are fewer local processes, and it is closer to how a live call behaves. The cost is depending on hosted speech APIs.</p>\n<h2>What I would measure next</h2>\n<p>The worker already logs STT wall time and LLM time to first token. I have not published an end-to-end latency comparison between the two stacks yet, and that is the obvious next step.</p>\n<p>The code is in the <a href=\"https://github.com/AdityaKundra/Voice-prototypes-two-stacks-\">Voice Assistant Lab repository</a>.</p>\n",
